  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1013 PM MDT Wed Oct 14 2026\n\nVery strong winds and much cooler temperatures are coming to \nSouthern New Mexico and Far West Texas Thursday night and Friday.\nA low pressure circulation in Colorado will exit the Rockies to \nthe east Thursday morning. A significant push of cooler air from \nCanada will fill in behind the system and move further south. A \nvery strong 1041 mb surface high is driving this air mass \nsouthward along the Front Range into New Mexico. \n\nThe cold front boundary will reach the local area by late \nThursday afternoon, reaching the El Paso and Las Cruces metro \nareas Thursday evening. There is tight pressure gradient packing \nand a low level jet with this front that will result in strong \nnortheasterly to easterly winds spreading across the area \nThursday evening through Friday afternoon. Sustained wind speeds \nof 30 to 40 mph with wind gusts exceeding 60 mph are expected \nbegin Thursday evening and lasting into Friday for Otero, El Paso,\nand Hudspeth Counties. A High wind warning has been issued for the\npreviously mentioned counties along with Hidalgo and southern \nGrant counties where wind speeds will reach warning criteria on \nFriday. \n\nThe remainder of the forecast area will experience strong \nwinds reaching wind advisory levels with  gusts to 50 mph, but \nare expected to remain below wind warning levels. Blowing dust is \nlikely with the strong winds, especially in El Paso and Hudspeth \ncounties along with southern portions of Dona Ana, Luna counties \nbeginning late Thursday evening. Blowing dust is also expected \nalong the playas in Hidalgo county on Friday. \n\nMuch cooler temperatures are also expected Friday into Saturday \nwith day time high temperatures struggling to reach the lower 60s \nin the lowlands. Many areas will see temperatures as much as 12 to\n16 degrees below normal Friday and Saturday. Freezing \ntemperatures are expected for the mountains Friday and Saturday \nmornings. \n\nChances for precipitation through Saturday remain near zero. A\npotential moisture surge into the area, hinted at by previous \nmodel forecasts, has shifted south and is not expected to be a \nfactor in our local weather pattern through the weekend. \n\nThe long term outlook for Sunday and much of next week looks\nwetter and warmer. A weakening low pressure system off of the Baja\nPeninsula will move into the desert southwest, pulling subtropical\nmoisture into the region as early as Sunday afternoon. There will\nbe a slight chance of showers for the bootheel on Sunday afternoon\nwith a greater chance of showers across the region starting Monday\nafternoon through next Thursday. The main driver for the precip\nwill be a deep low pressure system dropping down along the west\ncoast before moving inland and through the region on Thursday.\nThere will be a chance of rain Monday afternoon through next\nThursday.",
